  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+(6x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6x)]}_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/faa31c8261818c5c978d984a9b90a13e58794b02ff6f47e868ad6d5a06b7f71a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
